# Up the Entry - a coal round in the last week before Christmas, and three small decisions that accumulate.
# Written for the library. Deliberately multi-decision: ten stories in a row before this one had a single crux,
# and a story with one decision has an inert middle.

title: Up the Entry
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
The yard is four hundredweight of house coal and a hundred of smokeless, which is a light load
for the week before Christmas, because everybody who was going to order has ordered.
You have the cart, which is a flatbed Transit with a rotten tailgate, and you have Ray, who is
nineteen and on his second winter and who loads from the wrong end unless he is told.
-> the_loading

== the_loading ==
The book for today is six drops and the two that matter are at opposite ends of the round: the
Hall, which wants four hundredweight and is seven miles out past the reservoir, and then four
small ones in the town, two hundredweight between them.
The light goes at twenty past four. The Hall's housekeeper has already rung once.
* Do the Hall first and the town on the way back. -> big_first
* Do the four small ones first and the Hall last. -> small_first
* Split it: Ray takes the town in the car, you take the Hall. -> split_it

== big_first ==
You go out first, seven miles on roads that have not been gritted, and the gate to the Hall is
half a mile up a track with a slope on it that the Transit does not like in the wet.
The housekeeper meets you at the yard door in an apron with flour on it and counts the sacks
while Ray stacks them, which is the arrangement she has had with this round for eleven years.
-> the_town

== small_first ==
The four small drops take an hour and forty minutes, which is twenty minutes longer than you
thought, because the third one is an old woman who wants the sacks put in the outhouse and the
outhouse has a step.
It is ten past three when you turn for the reservoir, and the light is going, and the Hall's
housekeeper rings the yard while you are on the road and nobody is in the office to answer it.
-> the_hall_late

== the_hall_late ==
You get to the Hall at twenty past four with the light gone and the track slippery and the
housekeeper standing in the yard door with her coat on over the apron.
She takes it. She is not pleased and she takes it, and she says one sentence about next year while
Ray stacks, and the sentence is fair and you accept it, and you are back on the reservoir road at
five and home at twenty past, which is the latest this round has finished in four years.
-> END

== split_it ==
You split it, which you have only done twice before, and Ray takes the car and the town list and
goes, and you take four hundredweight and the hill.
It works better than it has any right to. He is back at the yard by four with the sheet signed
and the money in the tin, and you are at the Hall by half past three, and when you come down the
track the two of you pass on the reservoir road and he flashes his lights, which is not something
he has ever done before and which you think about for the rest of the evening.
-> the_town

== the_town ==
The town drops are all on the same estate and the estate is one-way with a barrier that gives
trouble, and at the second house the old man wants the coal in the bunker rather than on the
path, and the bunker is through the kitchen.
At the fourth house nobody is in, which happens, and you leave the sacks stacked by the door with
the ticket under the top one, and as you are getting back in the van Ray says the thing he has
been saving up.
ray: That's all right, that, in't it. Leaving it.
-> the_question

== the_question ==
Two hundredweight of coal left in a doorway and a ticket under the sack and a house that has been
on the round since before you had it, and the money has been paid by standing order since 1998.
It is twenty past four and there is one drop left, and it is the one up the entry.
* Agree with him. It is the arrangement and it has always been the arrangement. -> the_arrangement
* Tell him the arrangement exists for the houses that pay and this one does. -> the_paying
* Leave it, and say nothing, because he will find out in eleven years. -> the_silence

== the_arrangement ==
ray: Right. I just thought I'd ask.
you: Ask in eleven years.
He laughs at that and gets in and slams the door the way he slams everything, and the two of you
go up to Fell Street in the dark and the traffic, and it occurs to you, in the way things occur
while you are driving, that he asked because somebody had told him it was not all right, and that
somebody was probably his mother, and that he had asked you to settle an argument he had at home.
-> the_entry

== the_paying ==
you: It's not for the ones that pay. It's for the ones that aren't in and have paid anyway.
ray: Aye, well. You'd know.
He says it without any edge and he is quiet for a mile, and then he asks the other thing, which is
whether he can do the February on his own when you have your knee done, and you say yes before you
have thought about it, and the two of you drive up to Fell Street with that between you instead.
-> the_entry

== the_silence ==
You say nothing, which is what you have done with every other nineteen-year-old who has worked
this round, because the eleven-year answer is the true one and it does not fit in a van.
He watches you not answer, and he does not ask again, and over the following fortnight the two of
you settle into a shape where he copies you rather than asks you, which is better for the coal and
worse for him and you know both of those things.
-> the_entry

== the_entry ==
Fell Street is a terrace with a passage up the side of number 9, and the passage is what everybody
in the trade calls an entry, and it is thirty-one inches wide and it goes up eleven steps and turns
twice, and the coal house is at the top of it.
There are two hundredweight for number 9 and the entry has been the arrangement since the coal
house was built. The cart cannot get near it. Your shoulder is what you have.
-> the_entry_two

== the_entry_two ==
It is ten to five and dark and the fog is coming down off the park, and you have done this entry
about a thousand times and there are two ways to do it.
* Bag it small and take it up four trips, the way you always have. -> four_trips
* Take a hundredweight a trip and do it in two, and mind the turn. -> two_trips
* Leave it at the mouth of the entry and knock, and let her say what she wants. -> the_knock

== four_trips ==
Four trips of fifty pounds with the sack on your shoulder and your hand on the wall at the turn,
and it takes eleven minutes, and the coal house at the top is dry and there is a light on in the
kitchen and she has put a cup of tea on the step for when you come down, which she has done since
you took the round over.
On the fourth trip you put the last sack in and stand up straight, and your shoulder says the
thing it says every December, and you are fifty-one and you have eleven more years of this to
get through if you are lucky, and the tea is still hot.
-> ending_the_cup

== ending_the_cup ==
Four trips, eleven minutes, tea on the step, and the last sack in the coal house, and number 9
written off the sheet in the van and the money in the tin.
The fog comes down properly while you are driving home and you do the last two miles at twenty,
and you are in the house by half five and your knee is fine and the shoulder is not, and eleven
more years of this is what you have told yourself you want, and on a December evening with a hot
cup behind you it is the truth and you know it.
-> END

== two_trips ==
Two trips of a hundredweight with the sack on your shoulder and your hand on the wall, and on the
second one at the turn you catch the sack on the corner and it splits along the seam, and about
twenty pounds of coal goes down eleven steps in the dark and you spend a quarter of an hour on
your knees in it.
She comes out at the noise with a torch and does not say anything about the coal at all, and asks
you whether you are all right, and then sweeps it up herself while you are still apologising,
which is the part of the evening you will remember.
-> the_split

== the_split ==
You offered to make up the twenty pounds from tomorrow's load and she would not have it, and she
stood at the top of the entry with the torch while you stacked the last of it and she said the
sentence that everybody in the trade gets from somebody once or twice a decade.
her: You've done that entry when it was worse than this.
-> ending_the_torch

== ending_the_torch ==
She sweeps it up herself because she has had that entry longer than you have had the round, and
the twenty pounds is neither made up nor mentioned again, and the two of you stand at the top of
it in the fog with the torch between you and talk about the weather for four minutes, which is
what people in this town do instead of saying the other thing.
On the way down you count the steps, which you have never done, and there are eleven, and at the
bottom Ray is leaning on the van with his hands in his pockets and he does not laugh, which is
the first time all day that he has not laughed at something he could have.
-> END

== the_knock ==
You leave the two sacks at the mouth of the entry on the pavement and knock, which is not the
arrangement and which is the thing you have been talking to Ray about all afternoon without
saying so.
She opens the door in her cardigan, looks at the sacks, and looks at you, and then says the
thing that settles the whole day, which is not about the coal.
her: Are you not stopping for a cup, then.
-> ending_the_door

== ending_the_door ==
She leaves the sacks where they are and takes you through to the kitchen instead, and there is a
cup and there is a chair, and the coal gets carried up the entry an hour later by the two of you
together with her holding the torch and calling the turns.
Which is not the arrangement. The arrangement is thirty-one inches and eleven steps and a man on
his own, and it exists because the coal house was built at the top of the entry in 1903 and
somebody has to get the coal up there, and none of that is a reason to refuse a cup of tea from
a woman who has been on this round since before you had it.
The van is at the mouth of the entry with the tailgate down and the fog on it, and the street is
quiet, and you are in a kitchen at ten past five on a Thursday in December being asked about
your knee.
-> END
